  "text": "맠\n\n__)\n\n\n.rcParams ID:Web Site\n\n.rcParams ID:Web SiteTo answer this question, let's break down the problem into smaller steps and solve using trigonometry. Visualizing the problem will help us understand what is going on.\n\n1. **Understanding the Setup:**\n\n- We have a cylinder placed upside down on a horizontal plane.\n- A wire is leaning from the top of the cylinder down to a point on the plane. The wire forms a 60-degree angle with the horizontal plane.\n- A sphere is placed 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane.\n- The sphere just touches the wire and has a radius of 2 cm.\n\n2. **Visualizing the Diagram:**\n\nImagine a horizontal plane with a top of a cylinder at point Y and base at point Z. The wire BC (with B and C on the plane) forms a 60-degree angle with the horizontal. Point D (where the sphere touches the wire) is 10 cm from YZ. The sphere and the wire just touch each other.\n\n3. **Mathematical Setup:**\n\n- Since the sphere touches the wire and we know the radius of the sphere is 2 cm, we denote the radius of the cylinder as \\(r\\).\n- We need to find the radius of the cylinder.\n  \n       \\[\n       r = r \n              \\begin{cases}\n                2\\, \\text{cm}, & \\text{Radius of the sphere} \\\\\n                \\text{Unknown}, & \\text{Radius of the cylinder }\n              \\end{cases}\n           \\]\n\n- Analyzing the triangles involved:\n  - Triangle ABC is not directly relevant, but we can use right triangles for this analysis.\n  - Consider triangle XYZ which is a right triangle.\n\nLet's assume the base of triangle XYZ is the diameter of the cylinder, and the height extends perpendicularly to the base. Since cosine of \\(\\theta\\) (which is angle = 60 degrees in this scenario) is adjacent over hypotenuse, we can relate the right angles using triangle relationships:\n\n- Use the cosine function:\n  \\[\n  \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{\\frac{r}{2}}{height} = \\frac{r}{2 \\cdot height} ~~ \\sinleft(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{r}{2 \\cdot height}\n  \\]\n\n=}\n    \n\nThe length of the wire \\(w\\) can be obtained:\n\\[\nw=\\sqrt{14+4^2}=\\sqrt{14+16}=\\sqrt{30}\n\\]\n\nThe length is:\n\\[\nw=\\sqrt{30}\n\\]\n\nSubstitution gives:\n\\[\n\\frac{r}{3(2)^2} = \\frac{w}{3}\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{equation*}\n\\]",
